er of  exhibitions that really only exist in  people's memories or in archival  materials so one of them is a history of  a neighborhood to show how the state  made housing for workers a so called  workers new village I have an exhibition  on for children in the 1960s explaining  to them what was super stitch isn't and  what was science juxtaposing those two  behaviors or ways of thinking and then I  have two chapters one of them is about  class education in the years leading up  to the Cultural Revolution and one is on  red guard exhibitions and so between  those examples I have things that are  very well known and things that would  would disappear if we didn't have a  chance to think about them and so with  those examples I really want to bring  people behind the scenes at the museum  not just what was elective visit these  places but how are they put together and  how do they reflect the politics of that  period okay and what led you to be  interested in this that's a good  question I think I was first really  interested in historic preservation  so the preservation of the built  environment of monuments and that  actually goes back to my time as a Yale  undergraduate so almost 20 years ago I  was a sophomore and I took my first  Chinese history class and Jonathan  Spence had a lecture about leung  che-cheung who was he was a Chinese  first architectural historian he studied  at the University of Pennsylvania and in  the early 20th century he and his wife  who was an art historian they went  around China trying to catalog and  preserve ancient buildings in the middle  of wartime and so that was a really  inspiring story for me and I was  interested in historic preservation then  I got to the archive and I discovered  that there's actually a lot of material  about not just buildings temples  historic monuments but about art and  artifacts and so this I think it's  useful here to think about  the Chinese word for cultural relic so  the word is one rule so culture + object  cultural relic and so that includes both  kinds buildings + objects and that  really brought me into the study of  cultural preservation that way and I  ended up writing a dissertation about  Antiquities role in revolution ok and  how did you do the research it must have  been fascinating for you to kind of go  behind the scenes and take a look at  these artifacts and you also did  interviews with people as well - so tell  us about the research so I think the  research proceeded in stages and I like  to talk about this because I think for  graduate students they think it all has  to be complete like a whole book and to  start out with so I started out using  archives mostly the Shanghai Municipal  archive but some district archives as  well and I used the archival materials  to really give background in context to  create the history of the bureaucracy  that took care of culture during the  20th century and how did they define  what was a cultural relic and what  wasn't okay  then in my first job I started to think  of myself more as a cultural historian  and a cultural historian will look at  texts like a movie a book these the that  cultural product becomes a text to read  and so in this way I think I started to  think about the exhibitions as texts and  then finally as you said I got to do a  lot of interviews and do oral history  and that really helped me make the  project come alive to talk to people who  are curators who visited museums and  really put some sense of what it was  like to receive and understand these  exhibitions not just what was the  curators intent okay when you were  talking to some of these people were  there any surprises or things you didn't  expect that came out of those interviews  I think I could come up with a couple of  examples of what was surprising or what  I didn't  expect for some people I didn't expect  how much the cultural revolutions of 66  to 76 this big tumultuous political  event I didn't expect how much it was  taboo for some people and I was it taboo  for example talking asking about what  happened for example someone might say  something like oh it's not really clear  we don't have any records about this  when you having been in the archive I  knew actually there are records so that  is one example  so still to this day people did not want  to speak about that time I think it  really depends on what their role was  and how they feel about it to give you a  different kind of example I interviewed  someone who was a curator of paintings  in the Shanghai Museum and one thing  that surprised me about interviewing her  was how proud she was of the work she  did and the sense of responsibility that  she had to history so I have a chapter  about how people in the museum preserved  objects from destruction and I think I  asked her about things like how did you  feel about revolution and the answers  that I got actually spoke more to her  role as a professional as somebody whose  job as a museum worker was to preserve  these objects for for posterity and so  that was something that was really  interesting that came out of interviews  okay and curating revolution to curate  revolution help us understand what that  actually means so that's the book's  title curating revolution and so what I  try to do my conclusion is to think  beyond what we how we usually think  about curating so we usually think about  curating as just selecting some things  and putting them up when I was working  in the archives I found some interesting  documents around how to make a factory  exhibition in the 1960s and the memo  where the directive was it's not enough  just to put things on display  there's a whole second half and that's  making sure people understand it there's  an educated aspect of it and that they  understand it in the right way okay so  when I think about what it means to  curate revolution I talk about it in  terms of collecting in terms of  narration and then finally in terms of  ritual so collecting is putting together  a collection you don't have you can't  make a display unless you actually have  things to put on display so what is it -  not just collect things that have to do  with the Revolution or things that have  to do with our history but also at the  grassroots things that came from  people's ordinary lives how do you make  a village exhibition how do you make a  school exhibition so collection is just  the first step  narration is also a little more  complicated we think about narration as  the things that are on the walls the  text of the museum we think about the  things that the docent might have said  and we actually have texts what they  said how they were supposed to ask  questions or answer questions but there  are there are a couple of other elements  that don't show up in other studies of  museums one of them is actually going  down and interviewing people who  experienced a historical event and then  using those kind of channeling them or  using them as part of the docent script  and then one other piece that came out  that was really exciting was to have  handwritten notes in the archive from  the docent saying how was it how did  they perform when they were in the  exhibition space so narratives become  really important and then finally I  think the exhibition's in my study  really function as a ritual space mm-hmm  when we think about if we were in New  Haven and we went to the British art  museum or the university art gallery we  think of an individual going by  themselves because they're interested  but in the MAL period it was really  organized you would go with you're  working it it was very formal and you  might be asked to recite slogans at the  beginning or the end of the exhibit you  might be you might hear stories during  the exhibition or you might be called  upon to tell  your own story and then afterward you  would have a meeting in which you would  talk about the proper way to understand  the exhibit so exhibitions in the Maoist  period really become much more of a  ritual space in a way that I think is a  little bit foreign to an American  cultural context definitely in fact you  use the word ritual I would use the word  almost indoctrination in support of Mao  and what was going on politically during  that time correct so I'm curious  most of the exhibits were they  supportive of Mao and what was going on  there was there anything else that kind  of spoke against Mao and what was going  on that did not support the politics of  the day then hmm that's a really good  question this is a very politicized  moment and I think when you said  indoctrination the word that I was  thinking of is propaganda yeah these are  you know absolutely propaganda and or  techniques of propaganda something that  I call my book participatory propaganda  and so it's not just reading a newspaper  or looking at a book these are  interactive spaces and contexts so was  there anything ever against no I didn't  encounter anything against Mao in my  research but I think what's interesting  is you see the behind-the-scenes debates  like how do we present Mao in the early  1920s when the Communist Party was  founded Mao was not that important yet  but the politics of the 1960's said that  you should present Mao as if he were a  central figure so there's a lot of  tension behind the scenes I think that  there were probably some people who  disagreed with the exhibitions and you  don't always get to see those materials  but I did find a report having to do  with the class education exhibition and  it showed up in reports on  counter-revolutionary comments so you  know how at the end of an exhibition  there's often a book where you can leave  comments right and somebody had written  a comment saying your exhibition  very nice but it doesn't it doesn't  manage to cover up some of the  sufferings that the people have faced in  the past few years and in that case he  was talking about famine and then he  wrote about his grandfather starving to  death and the famine and so those kinds  of little critiques you can imagine that  some people had when they went through  the exhibition but for the most part  those kinds of counter narratives aren't  recorded right and I can imagine the  reason for that would but they were  probably even if they had been would  they have been removed I would imagine  just to be supportive of what was going  on during the politics of the day there  so yes that is fascinating so ultimately  what would you like the reader to take  away from your book I have a couple of  conclusions at the end where I think  about how the MAL period is different so  one thing people might ask is how is  this different from before 1949 and and  how is this different from today's China  or how is it different from other  socialist countries how is it different  from the Soviet Union and so in my book  in my conclusion I talked about two  modes of exhibition or a culture one of  them is the mode of the state and power  and the other is the mode of estate and  revolution so the state and power part  is about exhibitions that function as  tools of political legitimacy the  Revolutionary history museum the art  museum the museum about modernization of  architecture housing and I think that  kind of exhibition existed before 49 and  it exists in all kinds of contexts the  exhibitions of the state in power I  think are really an innovation or  something new that comes up during the  MAL period and they actually date from  before 1949 and it exhibitions of State  and Revolution really teach people how  to participate in revolution so to give  an example this phenomenon of new  exhibitions I date back to the land  reform period  when the communist party was still a  guerilla movement it had these base  areas and they would have these very  simple exhibitions in the countryside  where they would just have a table out  and they would put objects on it to show  okay their peasants and landlords here's  a gun this shows that the landlord has  power or they might even invite invite  and this is very performative the  landlord to put on his finest clothes  and put him on display as an example of  his wealth and this is one of the ways  in which categories of class become  legible for ordinary Chinese people  what's a landlord what is a peasant what  is a counter-revolutionary and so one of  the biggest watersheds in 20th century  Chinese history is the Cultural  Revolution where you had people attacked  because of their class background and  made to undergo class struggle and so  one of the things I try to do in my book  is to show how exhibitions before the  Cultural Revolution used personal  possessions to label people as being of  a bad class background this person is a  former capitalist this person still has  capitalist desires and so then people  used those material possessions to label  people as having a bad class and these  have enormous consequences so I think  that's an example of showing how these  exhibits really taught people not just  about a political campaign but how to  take part in it themselves and I think  this is something that we see as a  product of the mail period that is not  often evident in China - anymore and why  do you think that is that's a good  question I so if we think about the  Chinese state today it's a very  confident powerful state that is drawing  on both the revolutionary tradition and  a national tradition I think that the  vast majority of exhibitions in China -  they are the exhibits of the state in  power if you go to Beijing you can go to  the National Museum of China these are  about these are exhibits about political  legitimacy the times when you have the  exhibits of the state and revolution you  have it's when the state goes into a  campaign mode so for example I think the  most classic one might be campaigns  against corrupt officials and in those  cases the techniques that they borrow  here are the here's all of the money  that this person embezzled or whatever  and here's their corrupt lifestyle those  are a throwback to the Mao era so when  the state goes into political campaign  mode I think you have the revival of  that kind of exhibition area culture but  I think for the most part and here's the  main difference the state when those  political campaigns are looking for  enemies the corrupt official for example  these are not enemies of the revolution  they're enemies of the state so I think  that shows how how very different the  Chinese state is today even when it  draws upon that revolutionary repertoire  okay fascinating thank you so much for  being here today and sharing some of  your work thank you for having me for  more information about Professor Howe  and her research please visit our  website at macmillan report jled you be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you

